{
  "description": "Network passenger routing (Phase 5F, schema 35) with a walking transfer and station operation modes. Two dead-end tracks 6400 units (100 m) apart: nodes 1-7 at y 1536 and 8-14 at y 7936, x 512 to 6656, joined by straight edges e1-e6 and e7-e12, each 1024 long. A (1536, 1536) and B (3584, 1536) stand on the northern track, B' (3584, 7936) and C (5632, 7936) on the southern, each with platforms on the 512 units either side of its node. First (A, B) and Second (B', C) run all day with one train each (One on e1, Two on e9, one car). From 07:00 A (residential) and C (office) each send 50,000 trips a day, and routing is network: the only way between them is a ride to B (or B'), a walk of 100 m to the other track's station (a passage, 15 min x 1.2 = 18 min transfer penalty and 72 s at 5 km/h; the Ci reference's transfer types and walking speed) and the other line. After 30 minutes a full train's 352 passengers from A, who got off at B at 26785 s, wait at B' and may board 120 s later (the reference's least change time, longer than the walk); A has released 3134 (1500 arrived at C, 1634 still on the way) and C 1443 (1094 arrived). Closing B' sends away everyone whose journey still needs it, counted at their origin: A's 1634 and C's 100 waiting at C for Second, while C's 249 who already walked to B (ready 120 s after getting off) ride on to A; nobody is released while A and C have no route. A station that does not exist cannot be closed. Reopened, with A under flow control, C sends passengers again (A may still be their destination) and A sends none. Released = waiting + riding + arrived + overflowed + abandoned at every station. Expected values were recorded from GameCore and checked by hand against these rules; the reference model (ReferenceWorld) routes directly only and skips this fixture.",
